{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>This is the thing, etc.</b> One needs to know what <b>the thing</b> is. And Rashi explained it as referring to the offering of Aaron. But this is not the plain sense of the verse's statement, which says ״תַּעֲשׂוּ״. And it appears that it intends to speak of the deed that the verse mentioned just above, as it is written ״וַיִּקְרְבוּ כָּל הָעֵדָה וַיַּעַמְדוּ <b>לִפְנֵי ה׳</b>״ — the verse was precise in saying <b>before HaShem</b> and did not say ״before the Tent of Meeting״; nor was it necessary to say this, since He already said just above ״וַיִּקְחוּ וְגוֹ׳ אֶל פְּנֵי אֹהֶל מוֹעֵד״. Rather, it intends to say that they prepared themselves in the order of perfection and spiritual attainment, and the arrangement of their stance was before HaShem — and this aspect is the attainment of the ultimate purpose of perfection, as David said (Psalms 16:8): ״שִׁוִּיתִי ה׳ לְנֶגְדִּי תָמִיד״, for through this he will not slip from his right hand, which is Torah, since a person's sitting and movements, etc., are not like his sitting, etc., before a king — and all the more so before the King of the kings of kings. And when Moses perceived this matter, he said to them: ״זֶה הַדָּבָר אֲשֶׁר צִוָּה ה׳ תַּעֲשׂוּ״ — meaning, HaShem commanded that you do this, that you always set in your minds that you are standing before HaShem, and the glory of HaShem will appear to you. Further, it may intend by the inclusive force of saying <b>before HaShem</b> that there was no barrier separating between them and HaShem, as it is written (Isaiah 59:2): ״עֲוֹנוֹתֵיכֶם הָיוּ מַבְדִּילִים״ etc., and this declares that they were whole with HaShem. Accordingly, his saying ״זֶה״ about the deed mentioned just above is precisely correct." "<b>After</b> I had written this, a midrash of our Sages of blessed memory (Numbers Rabbah 12:8) came to my hand, and this is their language: ״זֶה הַדָּבָר״ — what is ״זֶה הַדָּבָר״? It concerns circumcision, as it says: ״זֶה הַדָּבָר אֲשֶׁר מָל יְהוֹשֻׁעַ״ (Joshua 5:4), end of quote. And the words of the midrash are sealed and closed: did Moses circumcise them on that very day, or did he command concerning circumcision on that very day?" "<b>Indeed,</b> the intent is concerning the orlah of their heart, which is the thing that separates between Israel and their Father in heaven, and also distances His awe from their heart. And He commanded to circumcise the orlah of their heart, and through this the glory of HaShem will appear to them — and these are our very own words." "Again, another midrash (Torat Kohanim, in the Yalkut) came to my hand, and this is their language: ״זֶה הַדָּבָר״ — Moses said to Israel: that evil inclination you must remove from your hearts, and you shall all be in one awe and in one counsel to serve before the Place, etc., and your service shall be uniquely dedicated before Him, as it is written: ״וּמַלְתֶּם אֶת עָרְלַת לְבַבְכֶם״ etc. (Deuteronomy 10:16), and if you do this — ״וְיֵרָא אֲלֵיכֶם כְּבוֹד ה׳״, end of quote. And these are our very own words."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 9, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:54:46.197901496Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}